export const T1={title:'The profession of physical therapy and the event',hook:'In 1918, a group of women called “reconstruction aides” worked in Army hospitals helping wounded World War I soldiers walk and use their arms again. Three years later they founded the professional association that became the APTA. Today physical therapists treat everyone from premature infants to Olympic athletes. The profession of PT is 14% of the test.',objectives:['Describe the Physical Therapy event, its test plan and its eight skills.','Trace the history of physical therapy and its education requirements.','Compare the roles of physical therapists, physical therapist assistants and aides.','Explain the patient/client management model and the settings where PTs work.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Physical Therapy has two rounds. **Round One** is a **50-question test in 60 minutes**: profession of PT **14%**, legal and ethical considerations **10%**, musculoskeletal conditions **16%**, neuromuscular conditions **16%**, cardiopulmonary conditions **14%**, integumentary conditions **14%** and pediatrics and older adults **16%**. References are *DHO: Health Science* (Simmers), Pierson and Fairchild’s *Principles and Techniques of Patient Care*, Pagliarulo’s *Introduction to Physical Therapy* and the APTA “PT Moves Me” skill videos.',
'Top scorers advance to **Round Two**: **I** ambulating with a gait belt (5 minutes), **II** standard walker (9), **III** cane (8), **IV** crutches (7), **V** passive range of motion on one side (10), **VI** cold application with an ice bag or pack (6), **VII** supine-to-sitting transfer (5) and **VIII** donning and removing transmission-based isolation garments (5). Bring photo ID, a gait belt (or use HOSA’s), a gown, two pairs of non-latex gloves and a mask. You need **70%** on the combined skills to be recognized; ties go to the higher test score.',
{t:'table',caption:'Written test plan',head:['Area','Weight'],rows:[['Musculoskeletal conditions','16%'],['Neuromuscular conditions','16%'],['Pediatrics and older adults','16%'],['Profession of PT','14%'],['Cardiopulmonary conditions','14%'],['Integumentary conditions','14%'],['Legal and ethical considerations','10%']]}]],
['History of physical therapy',[
'Physical treatments such as massage, exercise and hydrotherapy are ancient, but modern physical therapy grew out of crises. **Polio** epidemics in the early 1900s created a need for muscle re-education, and **World War I** created the **reconstruction aides**. In **1921**, Mary McMillan and colleagues founded the **American Women’s Physical Therapeutic Association**, which became the **American Physical Therapy Association (APTA)** in 1947. World War II and the polio epidemics of the 1940s and 1950s expanded the profession further.',
'Education rose over time from certificates to bachelor’s and master’s degrees. Today every accredited entry-level program awards the **Doctor of Physical Therapy (DPT)**, and graduates must pass the **National Physical Therapy Examination (NPTE)** and obtain a **state license**. **Physical therapist assistants (PTAs)** earn an **associate degree**, pass a national exam and are licensed or certified in nearly every state. Programs are accredited by **CAPTE**.',
order('Milestones in order',['Polio outbreaks spur muscle re-education','Reconstruction aides serve in World War I','APTA’s forerunner is founded (1921)','Name becomes APTA (1947)','Post–World War II and polio-era growth','DPT becomes the entry-level degree'])]],
['The PT team',[
'**Physical therapists (PTs)** examine patients, make a **physical therapy diagnosis**, set goals, write the **plan of care**, provide interventions, re-evaluate and discharge. **PTAs** provide interventions under the PT’s direction and supervision but **do not** perform initial evaluations, change the plan of care or discharge patients. **PT aides** are unlicensed workers who prepare treatment areas, transport patients and assist with tasks under **direct (on-site) supervision**; they do not provide skilled interventions independently.',
'PTs work with physicians, nurses, **occupational therapists** (daily activities and upper-body function), **speech-language pathologists**, **athletic trainers**, orthotists and prosthetists. Physical therapy belongs to the **therapeutic services** career pathway, along with occupational therapy, nursing and pharmacy, because these professions treat patients over time to improve health.',
sort('PT, PTA or aide?',['Physical therapist','Physical therapist assistant','PT aide'],[['Performs the initial evaluation',0],['Writes and changes the plan of care',0],['Provides exercise interventions under the plan',1],['Cleans equipment and prepares treatment tables',2],['Discharges the patient',0],['Modifies exercise within the plan and reports progress',1]])]],
['Patient/client management',[
'APTA’s **patient/client management model** has six elements: **examination** (history, systems review and tests and measures such as strength, range of motion and balance), **evaluation** (clinical judgment about the findings), **diagnosis** (a movement-related diagnosis), **prognosis** (expected improvement and time frame), **intervention** (exercise, functional training, manual therapy, modalities, education) and **outcomes**. Re-examination happens throughout.',
'The World Health Organization’s **ICF model** looks at **body structures and functions** (such as knee range of motion), **activities** (walking, climbing stairs) and **participation** (returning to work or sports), along with personal and environmental factors. Good goals are **functional**, measurable and time-bound: “The patient will walk 150 feet with a standard walker and supervision within 2 weeks.”',
order('Patient/client management model',['Examination','Evaluation','Diagnosis','Prognosis (with plan of care)','Intervention','Outcomes'])]],
['Practice settings',[
'PTs work in **acute care hospitals** (early mobility after surgery or illness, ICU care), **inpatient rehabilitation facilities**, **skilled nursing facilities**, **outpatient clinics** (orthopedics and sports), **home health**, **schools** (helping students participate in education), **pediatric clinics**, **sports and fitness** settings, **industrial and occupational health**, research and education. Specialties certified by the American Board of Physical Therapy Specialties include orthopedics, sports, neurology, pediatrics, geriatrics, cardiovascular and pulmonary, clinical electrophysiology, oncology, women’s health and wound management.',
'Job growth for PTs and PTAs is faster than average because of an aging population, chronic conditions such as diabetes and obesity and greater survival after injury and illness. Many PTs also complete **residencies** and **fellowships** after the DPT.',
match('Setting and focus',[['Acute care','Early mobility after surgery'],['Outpatient orthopedics','Sprains, post-operative knees, back pain'],['School-based','Access to education and campus mobility'],['Home health','Safety and function at home'],['Skilled nursing','Rehab before returning home'],['Sports','Return-to-play and injury prevention']])]],
['Communication and every-skill habits',[
'Every Round Two rating sheet shares a pattern: **obtain instructions from the PT (judge)**, assemble equipment, **knock** or request permission to enter and pause, provide **privacy**, greet and introduce yourself, **identify** the patient, perform **hand hygiene**, **explain and obtain consent**, perform the skill safely, and finish with hand hygiene, communication and **reporting to the PT**. Learning these “bookends” earns points in every skill.',
kase('Before the skill begins',{a:{text:'The scenario says: ambulate Mr. Hill, who has a weak left leg, 50 feet with a gait belt. What do you do first? (Scenario invented for practice.)',choices:[['Obtain instructions from the physical therapist, including the distance','b','Correct. Every skill starts with the PT’s instructions.',true],['Go straight to the patient’s room','b','Instructions come first.',false]]},b:{text:'At the door, what is next?',choices:[['Knock or request permission, pause, enter, greet, introduce yourself and identify the patient','c','Correct. Respect and identification.',true],['Enter and start applying the gait belt','c','Greet, identify and explain first.',false]]},c:{text:'You wash your hands, explain the skill and obtain consent. You are ready to begin.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Sample question',text:'HOSA asks which type of healthcare services includes physical therapy. The answer is **therapeutic services**.'}]]
],takeaways:['Round One: 50 questions in 60 minutes across seven areas; Round Two draws on eight skills and requires 70%.','Modern PT grew from polio care and WWI reconstruction aides; APTA traces to 1921; PTs earn a DPT and a license; PTAs earn an associate degree and work under PT supervision; aides are unlicensed.','Patient/client management: examination, evaluation, diagnosis, prognosis, intervention, outcomes; PT is a therapeutic service practiced in many settings.'],terms:[['DPT','Doctor of Physical Therapy, the entry-level degree for PTs.'],['Plan of care','The PT’s written goals and interventions for a patient.'],['Therapeutic services','Careers that treat patients over time to improve health.']],mcqs:[q('Which type of healthcare services includes the health science career pathway for physical therapy?','Therapeutic','Support','Diagnostic','Social','HOSA sample question.'),q('What degree do entry-level physical therapists earn today?','Doctor of Physical Therapy','Associate degree','Bachelor’s only','Certificate','DPT.'),q('Which task can a PTA NOT perform?','Initial evaluation','Therapeutic exercise','Gait training','Patient education','The PT evaluates.'),q('World War I workers who helped injured soldiers recover were called:','Reconstruction aides','Medics','Orderlies','Kinesiologists','They founded the profession.'),q('Which element comes first in the patient/client management model?','Examination','Intervention','Outcomes','Prognosis','Then evaluation.'),q('Which is a functional goal?','Walk 150 feet with a walker and supervision in 2 weeks','Increase knee flexion','Feel better','Do exercises','Measurable and functional.'),q('What exam must PT graduates pass?','NPTE','NCLEX','USMLE','PANCE','National Physical Therapy Examination.'),q('Which is the first step on every Round Two skill sheet?','Obtain instructions from the physical therapist','Apply the gait belt','Wash hands','Report to the PT','Instructions first.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the roles of the physical therapist, physical therapist assistant and PT aide, and explain how the patient/client management model guides a PT’s care for a patient recovering from knee surgery.',rubric:[['PT role',['evaluate','diagnosis','plan of care','discharge','dpt','license']],['PTA and aide',['pta','supervision','associate','aide','unlicensed','direct supervision']],['Management model',['examination','evaluation','diagnosis','prognosis','intervention','outcomes']],['Application',['range of motion','strength','walk','goal','functional','re-examination']]],model:'The physical therapist has a Doctor of Physical Therapy degree and a state license. The PT examines the patient, evaluates the findings, makes a movement-related diagnosis, determines the prognosis, writes and changes the plan of care, provides interventions and decides when to discharge. The physical therapist assistant has an associate degree, is licensed or certified and provides interventions under the PT’s plan and supervision but cannot perform the initial evaluation, change the plan of care or discharge. The PT aide is unlicensed and helps with non-skilled tasks such as preparing equipment and transporting patients under direct supervision. For a patient after knee surgery, the PT’s examination includes the history and measures of knee range of motion, strength, swelling, pain and walking ability. In the evaluation, the PT interprets these findings, makes a diagnosis such as impaired knee mobility and gait, and sets a prognosis with functional goals, for example walking without an assistive device and climbing stairs within six weeks. Interventions include exercise, gait training and education, possibly with cold application. The PT re-examines progress and measures outcomes such as return to work.'},sources:[RULES,APTA_MOVES,BLS_PT,BLS_PTA,CHOOSEPT]};

export const T2={title:'Legal and ethical practice and isolation garments (Skill VIII)',hook:'A patient asks a PT aide to adjust her exercise program because it hurts. A clinic manager wants every patient scheduled for the same number of visits regardless of need. A patient in contact isolation is waiting for therapy. Each situation involves law, ethics or safety—and the right answer is not always the easiest one. Legal and ethical considerations are 10% of the test.',objectives:['Explain state practice acts, licensure, supervision and direct access.','Apply the APTA Code of Ethics and patient rights, including informed consent and confidentiality.','Describe documentation requirements, including SOAP notes.','Don and remove transmission-based isolation garments following HOSA Skill VIII.'],sections:[
['Practice acts, licensure and supervision',[
'Each state has a **physical therapy practice act** that defines physical therapy, sets licensure requirements and says who may supervise whom. A state **board of physical therapy** enforces the act and can discipline licensees. Only licensed PTs may use the title “physical therapist” or the initials **PT** or **DPT**, and practicing without a license is illegal. PTAs work under the PT’s **general** or **direct** supervision as state law requires; aides require **direct, on-site** supervision.',
'**Direct access** means patients can see a PT without a physician referral. All 50 states and the District of Columbia allow some form of direct access, though many limit the number of visits or days before a referral is required, and some insurers require referrals. PTs must refer patients to physicians when findings suggest problems outside physical therapy, such as unexplained weight loss, fever or signs of a blood clot.',
match('Legal terms',[['Practice act','State law that defines PT practice'],['State board','Licenses and disciplines PTs and PTAs'],['Direct access','Seeing a PT without a physician referral'],['Direct supervision','Supervisor physically present on site'],['Scope of practice','What a licensed professional may legally do'],['Referral','Sending a patient to another provider when needed']])]],
['Ethics and patient rights',[
'The **APTA Code of Ethics** sets principles for PTs, and the **Standards of Ethical Conduct for the PTA** apply to assistants. Core values include **accountability, altruism, compassion and caring, excellence, integrity, professional duty and social responsibility**. PTs respect each patient’s **autonomy** (right to choose), act for the patient’s good (**beneficence**), avoid harm (**nonmaleficence**), treat people fairly (**justice**) and tell the truth (**veracity**). Billing for services not provided or recommending unnecessary visits is fraud and an ethical violation.',
'Patients have the right to **informed consent**: they must understand the treatment, its risks, benefits and alternatives and agree before it begins—and they may **refuse**. **HIPAA** protects health information; discuss patients only with those involved in their care and in private. The **Americans with Disabilities Act (ADA)** prohibits discrimination and requires reasonable accommodations, which shapes PT work in schools, workplaces and communities.',
sort('Ethical or unethical?',['Ethical','Unethical'],[['Explaining risks and alternatives before treatment',0],['Billing for a full session after 10 minutes of care',1],['Referring a patient with chest pain to a physician',0],['Discussing a patient’s diagnosis in the elevator',1],['Respecting a patient’s decision to decline an exercise',0],['Having an aide perform an evaluation to save time',1]])]],
['Documentation',[
'Documentation is a legal record, supports insurance payment and communicates with the team. A common format is the **SOAP note**: **Subjective** (what the patient reports—“My knee hurts 6/10 on stairs”), **Objective** (measurements and observations—knee flexion 0–95°, walked 100 feet with a walker), **Assessment** (the PT’s interpretation and progress toward goals) and **Plan** (next steps). Notes are timely, accurate, legible and signed; errors are corrected by a single line, dated and initialed—never erased.',
'Medicare and other payers require documentation of **medical necessity** and skilled care. Electronic health records are common, and every entry should be made by the person who provided the care. Students in the event verbalize that they **report the skill and observations to the PT**, the oral equivalent of documentation.',
sort('Which part of the SOAP note?',['Subjective','Objective','Assessment','Plan'],[['“My shoulder is stiff in the morning.”',0],['Shoulder flexion 0–120 degrees',1],['Progressing toward goal of reaching overhead shelf',2],['Continue strengthening 3 times per week',3],['Walked 200 feet with a cane, supervision',1],['Patient reports pain 4/10',0]])]],
['Transmission-based precautions',[
'**Standard precautions** apply to every patient. **Transmission-based precautions** add protection: **contact** precautions (gown and gloves) for germs such as MRSA, VRE and *C. difficile*; **droplet** precautions (mask) for influenza and pertussis; and **airborne** precautions (N95 respirator and an airborne infection isolation room) for tuberculosis, measles and chickenpox. PT equipment such as gait belts and walkers is either dedicated to the room or cleaned and disinfected after use.',
'Before entering, read the sign, gather supplies and perform hand hygiene. In Skill VIII you verbalize that **hands have been washed and jewelry removed**, then don a gown, mask and gloves, and later remove them so the contaminated outside never touches your skin or uniform.',
sort('Which precautions?',['Contact','Droplet','Airborne'],[['MRSA wound infection',0],['C. difficile diarrhea',0],['Influenza',1],['Pertussis',1],['Tuberculosis',2],['Measles',2]])]],
['Donning garments: Skill VIII steps 1–5',[
'**1.** Assemble equipment. **2.** Verbalize, “**Hands have been washed and jewelry removed.**” **3. Gown:** roll long sleeves above the elbows; lift the gown by placing your **hands inside the shoulders**; work your arms into the sleeves with a gentle twist; place your hands inside the **neckband**, adjust it and **tie at the back of the neck**; reach behind and fold the edges so your **uniform is completely covered**, then **tie the waistband**.',
'**4. Mask:** secure it **under the chin**, covering the mouth and nose; place elastic bands behind the ears or tie the **top ties first, then the bottom ties**. **5. Gloves:** put them on so they **cover the top cuff of the gown**. The judge then says the skill is completed and asks you to remove the PPE.',
order('Don in order',['Verbalize hands washed and jewelry removed','Roll up long sleeves','Lift the gown by the inside shoulders; arms in','Tie the neck, cover the uniform, tie the waist','Mask under the chin; top ties, then bottom ties','Gloves over the gown cuffs'])]],
['Removing garments: Skill VIII steps 6–9',[
'**6. Gloves:** grasp the **outside of one cuff** with the other gloved hand and pull the glove off **inside out**; place your **bare hand inside the cuff** of the second glove and pull it off inside out; discard both in the **infectious waste** container; perform hand hygiene. **7. Gown:** untie the **waist**, then the **neck**, loosening the gown at the shoulders while touching only the **inside**; slip the fingers of one hand inside the opposite cuff and pull the sleeve over your hand; use the gown-covered hand to pull down the other sleeve.',
'Ease your arms out, keeping the gown **in front of you**; with your hands inside, bring the shoulders together so the gown turns **inside out**, fold it in half, **roll it** and discard it. **8. Mask:** untie the **bottom ties first**, then the top (or remove from behind the ears), hold it **by the top ties only** and drop it in the infectious waste container. **9.** Perform hand hygiene.',
order('Remove in order',['Gloves inside out; hand hygiene','Untie the gown waist, then neck','Pull sleeves down over the hands, touching only the inside','Turn the gown inside out, fold, roll and discard','Mask: bottom ties first, hold by the top ties','Hand hygiene']),
flip('Skill VIII details',[['Gown lift','Hands inside the shoulders'],['Mask ties on','Top first, then bottom'],['Mask ties off','Bottom first, then top'],['Gloves','Cover the gown cuff'],['Gown off','Inside out, fold, roll'],['Disposal','Infectious waste container']])]]
],takeaways:['State practice acts define PT and licensure; PTAs work under PT supervision; aides need direct supervision; all states allow some form of direct access.','Ethics: autonomy, beneficence, nonmaleficence, justice, veracity and informed consent; protect privacy under HIPAA; SOAP notes document subjective, objective, assessment and plan.','Skill VIII: gown (hands inside shoulders, neck then waist), mask (top ties first), gloves over cuffs; remove gloves, gown inside out and rolled, mask by bottom ties then top, hand hygiene.'],terms:[['Practice act','A state law that defines and regulates a profession.'],['Informed consent','Agreement after learning a treatment’s risks, benefits and alternatives.'],['SOAP note','Documentation format: subjective, objective, assessment, plan.']],mcqs:[q('What does direct access mean?','Seeing a PT without a physician referral','Getting a direct flight to the clinic','A PTA evaluating patients','Free physical therapy','All states allow some form.'),q('Who can write and change the plan of care?','The physical therapist','The PTA','The aide','The front desk','PT responsibility.'),q('In a SOAP note, range of motion measurements belong in:','Objective','Subjective','Assessment','Plan','Measured data.'),q('Which ethical principle means respecting a patient’s right to choose?','Autonomy','Justice','Veracity','Nonmaleficence','Self-determination.'),q('How are mask ties secured when donning?','Top ties first, then bottom','Bottom first, then top','Only the bottom','They are not tied','Removal is the reverse.'),q('Which PPE is removed first in Skill VIII?','Gloves','Mask','Gown','Shoe covers','Then hand hygiene.'),q('Which precautions are used for tuberculosis?','Airborne','Contact','Droplet','Standard only','N95 and an isolation room.'),q('How should the gown be handled during removal?','Touch only the inside and roll it inside out','Pull it over the head by the outside','Shake it off','Leave it on the floor','Contain contamination.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how to don and remove transmission-based isolation garments following HOSA Skill VIII, and explain why the order and technique protect the clinician and other patients.',rubric:[['Preparation and gown',['hands washed','jewelry','sleeves','inside the shoulders','neck','waist','uniform covered']],['Mask and gloves',['under the chin','top ties','bottom','gloves','cuff']],['Removal technique',['gloves','inside out','hand hygiene','waist','neck','inside','roll','bottom ties','top ties']],['Reasoning',['contaminated','outside','skin','uniform','spread','infectious waste']]],model:'I assemble equipment and verbalize that my hands have been washed and jewelry removed, then roll long sleeves above my elbows. I lift the gown by placing my hands inside the shoulders, work my arms into the sleeves, place my hands inside the neckband and tie it at the back of the neck, then fold the edges behind me so my uniform is completely covered and tie the waist. I secure the mask under my chin over my mouth and nose and tie the top ties first and then the bottom ties, and I put on gloves that cover the top cuff of the gown so no skin is exposed. To remove, I take off the gloves first because they are the most contaminated: I grasp the outside of one cuff, pull it off inside out, put my bare hand inside the cuff of the other glove, pull it off inside out, discard them in the infectious waste container and perform hand hygiene. I untie the waist and neck ties, touching only the inside of the gown, pull each sleeve down over my hands, ease out of the gown while holding it in front of me, turn it inside out, fold and roll it and discard it. I remove the mask by untying the bottom ties first, then the top, handle it only by the top ties and drop it in the infectious waste, then perform hand hygiene. This order keeps the contaminated outside surfaces away from my skin and uniform and prevents carrying germs to other patients.'},sources:[RULES,APTA_ETHICS,APTA_DIRECT,CDC_ISO]};

export const T3={title:'Body mechanics, bed mobility and gait-belt ambulation (Skills I and VII)',hook:'Helping a patient sit up and take their first steps after surgery is one of the most rewarding parts of physical therapy—and one of the riskiest for both patient and clinician. Falls injure patients, and poor lifting technique injures healthcare workers. Skills I and VII test whether you can move a patient safely, step by step.',objectives:['Apply body mechanics and safe patient handling principles.','Transfer a patient from supine to sitting following HOSA Skill VII.','Apply a gait belt and ambulate a patient following HOSA Skill I.','Respond correctly if a patient begins to fall.'],sections:[
['Body mechanics and safe handling',[
'**Body mechanics** means using the body efficiently and safely. Keep a **wide base of support** with feet apart—often one foot in front of the other (**anteroposterior** or staggered stance)—to increase stability. Keep the load **close to your center of gravity**, **bend at the hips and knees** rather than the waist, keep your **back straight**, tighten your abdominal muscles, and **pivot with your feet** instead of twisting your trunk. Push or pull rather than lift whenever possible.',
'NIOSH recommends that healthcare workers avoid manually lifting more than about **35 pounds** of a patient’s weight; heavier transfers require **mechanical lifts** or more staff. Before any transfer, **lock** the bed or wheelchair, adjust the bed to a safe height, explain each step, use a clear signal such as a **count of three** and let the patient do as much as they can—this builds strength and independence.',
sort('Safe or unsafe body mechanics?',['Safe','Unsafe'],[['Staggered stance with a wide base',0],['Twisting the trunk while lifting',1],['Bending at the knees and hips',0],['Holding the patient at arm’s length',1],['Counting to three so both move together',0],['Manually lifting a patient who cannot bear weight alone',1]])]],
['Supine to sitting: Skill VII',[
'Skill VII has 19 steps in 5 minutes. After obtaining instructions, assembling equipment, knocking, greeting, identifying, hand hygiene and explaining with consent: **8.** Move the patient close to the edge of the bed, using the **length of the patient’s thigh** as a guide to where the buttocks should be. **9.** Ask the patient to **look in the direction of the movement** when rolling. **10.** Roll the patient to **side-lying** with the legs **partially flexed**. **11.** Place one arm **under the patient’s bottom shoulder**, reaching toward the scapula, and the other **behind the bent knees**.',
'**12.** Tell the patient that on the **count of three** they will **push up** with one or both arms. **13.** Place your feet in an **anteroposterior position** to widen your base and avoid twisting. **14.** On three, **elevate the trunk** by lifting under the shoulders while **moving the legs off the bed**—the legs act as a counterweight. **15.** **Support the pelvis** as the patient reaches sitting. **16.** **Never leave the patient sitting unattended or unsupported**; watch for dizziness. **17–19.** Hand hygiene, communication and reporting to the PT.',
order('Supine to sitting',['Move the patient toward the edge (thigh length as a guide)','Patient looks toward the movement','Roll to side-lying with knees partly bent','One arm under the bottom shoulder, one behind the knees','Staggered stance; count to three','Lift the trunk while swinging the legs off the bed','Support the pelvis; never leave the patient unsupported'])]],
['Applying the gait belt: Skill I steps 1–15',[
'Skill I has 26 steps in 5 minutes. **1.** Obtain instructions from the PT. **2.** Assemble equipment. **3.** Knock or request permission and **pause**. **4.** Provide privacy. **5.** Greet and introduce yourself. **6.** Identify the patient. **7.** Hand hygiene. **8.** Explain and obtain consent. If the patient is in bed: **9.** lock the bed and lower the near side rail; **10.** help them sit and put on a robe if needed.',
'**11.** Check that the gait belt is the **correct size** and place it around the **waist over clothing**. **12.** Position the buckle **slightly off center in the front**, smooth and free of wrinkles. **13.** Tighten it so it fits **snugly**, secure the clasp and **slip your fingers under the belt** to check it is not too tight. **14.** Put on **non-skid** shoes or slippers. **15.** Make sure the patient’s **feet are on the floor** and they are close to the edge of the bed or chair.',
chk('Where should the gait belt buckle be?',['Slightly off center in the front','Directly over the spine','Under the arm','On the side over the hip bone'],'Off center avoids pressure on the abdomen and is easy to reach.')]],
['Standing and walking: Skill I steps 16–19',[
'**16a.** Face the patient with a **broad base of support** and grasp the **loops** on the sides of the belt or place your hands **under the sides of the belt** (underhand grip). **16b.** Ask the patient to push against the bed or chair with their hands at your signal. **16c.** Bend your knees, give the signal, keep your back straight and **straighten your knees as the patient stands**. **17.** Support the patient in standing, keeping one hand on one side of the belt while moving the other to the loops or the back of the belt. **18.** Move the second hand to the back of the belt as you **move behind the patient**.',
'**19a.** Encourage the patient to **walk slowly** and use handrails if available. **19b.** Walk **slightly behind** the patient at all times with a **firm underhand grip** on the belt. **19c.** If the patient has a **weak side**, position yourself on the **weak side**. Watch for fatigue, dizziness, shortness of breath and unsteadiness, and walk the distance or time the PT ordered.',
order('Stand and walk',['Face the patient with a broad base; grasp the belt underhand','Patient pushes on the bed at your signal','Bend your knees, then straighten as the patient stands','Support in standing; move a hand to the back of the belt','Move behind the patient','Walk slightly behind on the weak side with a firm grip'])]],
['If the patient starts to fall: Skill I steps 20–26',[
'Do **not** try to hold up a falling patient—it can injure both of you. **20a.** Keep a **firm grip** on the belt. **20b.** Use your body to **brace** the patient, keep your back straight and **gently ease the patient to the floor**, protecting the **head**. **20c.** **Stay with the patient** and **call for help**. **20d.** Do not try to stand the patient up until help arrives and the patient has been examined. (The judge will say that help has arrived and the patient may return to bed or chair.)',
'**21.** Assist the patient back to the bed or chair after the ordered distance (or after a fall, when cleared). **22.** Remove the gait belt. **23.** Raise the side rail if indicated and observe **checkpoints**—call light within reach, bed low and locked, patient comfortable. **24.** Hand hygiene. **25.** Communicate appropriately. **26.** **Report** the skill and observations—including any fall—to the PT.',
order('Controlled fall',['Keep a firm grip on the gait belt','Brace the patient against your body with a straight back','Ease the patient gently to the floor, protecting the head','Stay with the patient and call for help','Do not stand the patient up until help arrives and the patient is checked','Report the fall to the PT'])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Gait belt ambulation',{a:{text:'Your patient has right-side weakness after a stroke. Where do you walk?',choices:[['Slightly behind on the right (weak) side with an underhand grip on the belt','b','Correct. Guard the weak side.',true],['In front of the patient, walking backward','b','Walk slightly behind on the weak side.',false]]},b:{text:'Halfway down the hall, the patient’s knees buckle. What do you do?',choices:[['Keep your grip, brace them against your body and ease them to the floor, protecting the head','c','Correct. A controlled descent prevents injury.',true],['Try to lift them back up by the belt','c','Lifting a falling patient risks injury to both of you.',false]]},c:{text:'You stay, call for help and report the fall to the PT after the patient is examined.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'clinical',title:'Orthostatic hypotension',text:'When patients sit or stand after lying down, blood pressure may drop and cause dizziness or fainting. Let the patient sit at the edge of the bed for a moment (dangling), ask how they feel and watch for pallor or sweating before standing.'}]]
],takeaways:['Body mechanics: wide staggered base, load close, bend hips and knees, straight back, pivot with the feet; use lifts for heavy transfers.','Skill VII: move to the edge, look toward the roll, side-lying with knees bent, arms under shoulder and knees, count to three, lift the trunk as the legs swing off, support the pelvis and never leave the patient unsupported.','Skill I: gait belt over clothing, buckle slightly off center, fingers fit underneath, non-skid footwear; stand on a signal, walk slightly behind on the weak side; if falling, ease to the floor, protect the head, stay and call for help.'],terms:[['Gait belt','A belt worn around the patient’s waist that gives the clinician a secure handhold.'],['Base of support','The area beneath a person that includes every point of contact with the floor.'],['Orthostatic hypotension','A drop in blood pressure on sitting or standing that can cause dizziness.']],mcqs:[q('Where should the clinician walk when ambulating a patient with a weak side?','Slightly behind on the weak side','In front of the patient','On the strong side','Several feet away','Guard the weak side.'),q('How do you check gait-belt tightness?','Slip your fingers under the belt','Pull until the patient complains','Look at it','Ask another patient','Snug but not too tight.'),q('Where is the gait belt placed?','Around the waist on top of clothing','Under the arms','Around the chest on skin','Around the hips under clothing','Over clothing.'),q('If a patient starts to fall, you should:','Ease them to the floor while protecting the head','Hold them up at all costs','Let go and step back','Lift them back to standing immediately','Controlled descent.'),q('In Skill VII, where is the patient positioned before sitting up?','Side-lying with knees partially flexed','Prone','Standing','Supine with legs straight','Roll first.'),q('Why move the legs off the bed while lifting the trunk?','The legs act as a counterweight','To stretch the hamstrings','To check reflexes','It is not needed','Makes sitting easier.'),q('What foot position widens your base and prevents twisting?','Anteroposterior (staggered) stance','Feet together','Standing on one foot','Toes pointed in','Per Skill VII.'),q('After a fall, when may the patient be helped up?','After help arrives and the patient has been examined','Immediately','When the patient asks','Never','Prevents further injury.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how you would help a patient move from lying supine to sitting on the edge of the bed and then ambulate with a gait belt, including what you would do if the patient began to fall.',rubric:[['Supine to sitting',['edge','thigh','look','side-lying','knees','shoulder','count','legs off','pelvis','unsupported']],['Gait belt',['correct size','waist','clothing','off center','snug','fingers','non-skid']],['Ambulation',['broad base','underhand','signal','straighten','behind','weak side','slowly']],['Fall response',['grip','brace','ease','floor','head','stay','call for help','report']]],model:'After obtaining instructions from the PT, assembling equipment, knocking, greeting and identifying the patient, performing hand hygiene and obtaining consent, I move the patient toward the edge of the bed, using the length of the thigh as a guide. I ask the patient to look in the direction of the roll, roll them to side-lying with the knees partly bent, place one arm under the bottom shoulder toward the scapula and the other behind the knees and stand in a staggered stance. On the count of three, the patient pushes up with their arms while I lift the trunk and swing the legs off the bed; I support the pelvis and never leave them unsupported, watching for dizziness. I check that the gait belt is the right size, place it around the waist over clothing with the buckle slightly off center in front, tighten it until my fingers just fit underneath and put on non-skid footwear. Facing the patient with a broad base and an underhand grip on the belt, I give a signal, bend my knees and straighten them as the patient pushes up to stand, then move behind the patient and walk slightly behind on the weak side. If the patient begins to fall, I keep a firm grip, brace them against my body with a straight back and ease them to the floor while protecting the head, stay with them, call for help and do not stand them up until they are examined; afterward I report the fall and my observations to the PT.'},sources:[RULES,APTA_MOVES,NIOSH_LIFT,CDC_STEADI]};

export const T4={title:'Assistive devices: walkers, canes and crutches (Skills II, III and IV)',hook:'An assistive device that is the wrong height can cause back pain, falls or nerve damage under the arms. A patient who slides a walker or leans on crutch pads can fall. Fitting devices correctly and teaching the right gait pattern are core PT skills—and Skills II, III and IV test all three devices.',objectives:['Fit walkers, canes and crutches to the correct height.','Teach walker ambulation following HOSA Skill II.','Teach two-point and three-point cane gaits following HOSA Skill III.','Teach four-point, three-point and two-point crutch gaits following HOSA Skill IV, and interpret weight-bearing orders.'],sections:[
['Weight-bearing orders and device choice',[
'Physicians and PTs specify how much weight a patient may place on an injured leg. **Non-weight-bearing (NWB)**: no weight at all. **Toe-touch or touch-down weight-bearing (TTWB/TDWB)**: the foot may touch the floor for balance only. **Partial weight-bearing (PWB)**: a set amount, such as 50% or 30 pounds. **Weight-bearing as tolerated (WBAT)**: as much as comfortable. **Full weight-bearing (FWB)**: no restriction.',
'Device choice depends on balance, strength and weight-bearing status. **Walkers** give the most stability (standard walkers must be lifted; rolling walkers have front wheels). **Crutches** allow NWB and PWB gaits but require good balance, arm strength and coordination. **Canes** offer the least support—**quad canes** have four small feet for more stability than a single-point cane. Before use, check the equipment: walker legs have secure **rubber tips**, crutch tips are not worn and axillary bars and hand grips are **padded**, and canes have a **rubber suction tip**.',
match('Weight-bearing terms',[['NWB','No weight on the leg'],['TTWB','Toe touches for balance only'],['PWB','A limited, specified amount'],['WBAT','As much as the patient can tolerate'],['FWB','Full weight allowed'],['Quad cane','Four-footed cane with a wider base']])]],
['The standard walker: Skill II',[
'Skill II has 25 steps in 9 minutes. After instructions (including distance), assembling equipment and **checking the walker** (secure rubber tips, no rough edges), knocking, greeting, identifying, hand hygiene and consent, apply the gait belt, put on non-skid footwear, help the patient to the edge of the bed with feet on the floor, tighten the belt and use an **underhand grasp** to help the patient stand. Position the walker and have the patient grasp the hand rests.',
'Check **height**: the hand rests are level with the **radial styloid process** (the wrist bone on the thumb side) with the arm relaxed, and the **elbows are flexed 25–30 degrees**. Notify the PT if it needs adjustment. Start with the patient **inside** the walker. Instruct: **lift** the walker and place it forward so the **back legs are even with the patient’s toes**; make sure **all four legs are on the floor** before stepping; **do not slide** the walker; shift weight forward slightly; and **walk “into” the walker** without shuffling. Walk to the side and slightly behind on the **weak side**, stay alert, return the patient to the chair, remove the belt, replace equipment, hand hygiene, communicate and report.',
order('Walker sequence',['Lift the walker','Place it forward—back legs even with the toes','All four legs firmly on the floor','Shift weight forward onto the walker','Step into the walker without shuffling','Repeat'])]],
['Canes: Skill III',[
'Skill III has 24 steps in 8 minutes. Obtain instructions and **which gait the PT taught**, check the cane’s **rubber suction tip**, then complete the usual entry, identification, footwear, edge-of-bed and gait-belt steps and help the patient stand. Instruct the patient to bear weight on the **unaffected leg**. Fit the cane: place it on the **unaffected (strong) side** about **6–8 inches from the foot**; the top is level with the **radial styloid process** with the arm relaxed, and the **elbow is flexed 25–30 degrees**.',
'**Three-point gait:** balance on the strong foot; move the **cane forward 12–18 inches**; move the **weak leg** forward; transfer weight to the weak leg and cane; bring the **strong leg** forward. **Two-point gait:** balance on the strong foot; move the **cane and the weak leg forward together**; keep the cane close to the body to avoid leaning; transfer weight to the cane; move the strong leg forward. Walk slightly behind on the weak side, remain alert, return the patient, remove the belt and report.',
order('Three-point cane gait',['Balance on the strong foot','Move the cane forward 12–18 inches','Move the weak leg forward','Shift weight to the weak leg and cane','Bring the strong leg forward']),
chk('On which side is a cane held?',['The unaffected (strong) side','The affected (weak) side','Either side','In front of the body'],'This widens the base and mimics a normal arm swing.')]],
['Fitting crutches: Skill IV',[
'Skill IV has 26 steps in 7 minutes. Check that the crutch **tips are not worn or torn** and the **axillary bars and hand rests are padded**. After the usual steps and the gait belt, instruct the patient **not to move the crutches too far forward** and **not to rest weight on the axillary bars**—pressure in the armpit can damage the **radial nerve** (“crutch palsy”). Weight is carried on the **hands**.',
'Check fit: place the crutch tips **4–6 inches in front** of the feet and **2–4 inches to the side**; leave a **1½–2 inch gap** (about **2–3 finger widths**) between the axilla and the axillary bar; and confirm each **elbow is flexed 25–30 degrees**. Notify the PT if adjustments are needed. Then help the patient stand with an underhand grasp on the belt, remind them to bear weight on the unaffected leg and position the crutches on the stronger side to begin.',
flip('Fit numbers',[['Walker and cane height','Level with the radial styloid'],['Elbow flexion','25–30 degrees for all devices'],['Cane placement','Strong side, 6–8 inches from the foot'],['Crutch tips','4–6 inches in front, 2–4 inches to the side'],['Axilla gap','1½–2 inches (2–3 finger widths)'],['Weight on crutches','On the hands, not the armpits']])]],
['Crutch gaits',[
'**Four-point gait** (most stable, slow; partial weight on both legs): right crutch, **left foot**, left crutch, **right foot**. **Three-point gait** (for NWB or PWB on one leg): advance **both crutches** and the **weak leg** together, transfer weight to the crutches, then advance the **strong leg**. **Two-point gait** (faster; requires more balance): move the **right foot and left crutch** together, then the **left foot and right crutch** together.',
'On **stairs**, remember “**up with the good, down with the bad**”: going up, the strong leg steps up first, followed by the crutches and weak leg; going down, the crutches and weak leg go first. Throughout Skill IV, remind the patient of the sequence, correct technique (no leaning on axillary bars, not advancing crutches too far), walk slightly behind on the weak side and stay alert.',
order('Four-point crutch gait',['Right crutch forward','Left foot forward','Left crutch forward','Right foot forward']),
sort('Which crutch gait?',['Four-point','Three-point','Two-point'],[['Right crutch, left foot, left crutch, right foot',0],['Both crutches and the weak leg, then the strong leg',1],['Right foot with left crutch, then left foot with right crutch',2],['Used for non-weight-bearing on one leg',1],['Slowest and most stable',0],['Faster pattern needing good balance',2]])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Device problems',{a:{text:'A patient with a walker slides it forward on the floor while walking. What do you say?',choices:[['“Please lift the walker and set all four legs down before stepping.”','b','Correct. Sliding a standard walker can cause falls.',true],['“Sliding is fine; it is faster.”','b','Standard walkers are lifted.',false]]},b:{text:'A patient on crutches says his armpits are numb. What is the likely cause?',choices:[['Leaning on the axillary bars; weight should be on the hands','c','Correct. Axillary pressure can injure nerves.',true],['The crutches are too short','c','Leaning on the pads is the common cause—check fit too.',false]]},c:{text:'You correct his technique, check the 2–3 finger gap and report to the PT.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Know NWB, TTWB, PWB, WBAT and FWB; walkers are most stable, then crutches, then canes; inspect tips and padding first.','Walker and cane handles are level with the radial styloid with 25–30° elbow flexion; lift the walker, back legs even with the toes, four legs down, step in; the cane goes on the strong side 6–8 inches out.','Crutches: tips 4–6 inches front and 2–4 inches side, 1½–2 inch axilla gap, weight on the hands; four-, three- and two-point gaits; “up with the good, down with the bad.”'],terms:[['Radial styloid process','The bony bump on the thumb side of the wrist used to set device height.'],['Weight-bearing as tolerated','The patient may put as much weight on the leg as is comfortable.'],['Three-point gait','A pattern in which the device and weak leg move together, then the strong leg.']],mcqs:[q('Where should walker hand rests be?','Level with the radial styloid process','At waist height','At the elbow','At shoulder height','Arm relaxed at the side.'),q('How much elbow flexion is correct with walkers, canes and crutches?','25–30 degrees','0 degrees','45–60 degrees','90 degrees','Allows push-off.'),q('Where are the walker’s back legs placed after lifting it forward?','Even with the patient’s toes','A full arm’s length ahead','Behind the heels','To the side','Per Skill II.'),q('A cane is placed how far from the foot?','6–8 inches on the unaffected side','2 inches on the affected side','12–18 inches behind','Touching the foot','Per Skill III.'),q('What gap is needed between the axilla and the crutch pad?','1½–2 inches (2–3 finger widths)','None','6 inches','4–6 inches','Prevents nerve compression.'),q('In the four-point gait, what follows the right crutch?','Left foot','Right foot','Left crutch','Both crutches','Right crutch, left foot, left crutch, right foot.'),q('Which gait suits non-weight-bearing on one leg?','Three-point','Four-point','Two-point','Swing-to with both feet','The weak leg is held off the floor.'),q('Going up stairs on crutches, which leads?','The strong leg','The crutches','The weak leg','Both legs together','Up with the good.')],frq:{prompt:'A patient has a sprained right ankle and is allowed partial weight-bearing. The PT taught a three-point crutch gait. Explain how you would check the crutches, fit them and teach the gait following Skill IV.',rubric:[['Check equipment',['tips','worn','padding','axillary','hand rests']],['Fit',['4-6 inches','2-4 inches','1 1/2','2 inch','finger','25-30','elbow']],['Instructions',['hands','axillary','too far','weight','unaffected','strong side']],['Three-point gait and guarding',['both crutches','weak leg','transfer','strong leg','behind','weak side','report']]],model:'I obtain instructions from the PT and confirm the three-point gait was taught, then check that the crutch tips are not worn or torn and that the axillary bars and hand rests are padded. After knocking, greeting and identifying the patient, performing hand hygiene, obtaining consent, putting on non-skid footwear and applying the gait belt snugly over clothing with the buckle slightly off center, I instruct the patient not to move the crutches too far forward and not to rest weight on the axillary bars, because pressure there can injure nerves; the weight goes on the hands. I check the fit with the tips 4–6 inches in front of and 2–4 inches to the side of the feet, a 1½–2 inch gap (2–3 finger widths) between the axilla and the bar and the elbows flexed 25–30 degrees, notifying the PT if adjustments are needed. I help the patient stand with an underhand grip on the belt and remind them to bear weight on the unaffected left leg. For the three-point gait, the patient advances both crutches and the right (affected) leg together, transfers weight forward onto the crutches—allowing only partial weight on the right foot—and then advances the left leg. I walk slightly behind on the weak right side, correct technique throughout, return the patient to the chair, remove the belt, perform hand hygiene and report to the PT.'},sources:[RULES,APTA_MOVES,CDC_STEADI]};

export const T5={title:'Musculoskeletal conditions and therapeutic exercise',hook:'Low back pain, knee arthritis, sprained ankles, rotator cuff tears and joint replacements fill outpatient clinics every day. Physical therapists treat them with carefully chosen exercise, manual therapy and education—often helping patients avoid surgery or recover faster after it. Musculoskeletal conditions are 16% of the test.',objectives:['Describe common musculoskeletal conditions, including sprains, strains, fractures, arthritis and joint replacement.','Explain the phases of tissue healing and how they guide treatment.','Compare passive, active-assisted, active and resistive exercise, including isometric, isotonic and isokinetic exercise.','Apply precautions after total hip and knee replacement.'],sections:[
['Soft-tissue injuries',[
'A **sprain** is a stretch or tear of a **ligament** (bone to bone), most often the lateral ankle. A **strain** is a stretch or tear of a **muscle or tendon** (muscle to bone), such as a hamstring strain. Both are graded **I (mild stretch)**, **II (partial tear)** or **III (complete tear)**. **Tendinopathy** describes painful, degenerated tendons (for example, the Achilles or patellar tendon), and **bursitis** is inflammation of a fluid-filled sac that reduces friction.',
'Early care often follows **PRICE** or **POLICE**: **P**rotection, **O**ptimal **L**oading (gentle, appropriate movement rather than complete rest), **I**ce, **C**ompression and **E**levation. PTs progress patients through range of motion, strengthening, balance (proprioception) and sport- or job-specific training. Common knee injuries include **anterior cruciate ligament (ACL)** tears, often from pivoting, and **meniscus** tears. Shoulder problems include **rotator cuff** tears and impingement.',
sort('Sprain or strain?',['Sprain (ligament)','Strain (muscle or tendon)'],[['Rolled ankle injuring the ATFL',0],['Pulled hamstring while sprinting',1],['ACL tear',0],['Low back muscle strain lifting a box',1],['Calf muscle tear',1],['MCL injury in the knee',0]])]],
['Fractures, arthritis and the spine',[
'**Fractures** are breaks in bone: **closed** or **open (compound)**, **stress** fractures from repeated loading and **pathologic** fractures from weakened bone. Healing takes about **6–12 weeks** in adults; PTs work within weight-bearing orders and restore motion and strength once healing allows. **Osteoporosis** (low bone density) increases fracture risk, especially of the hip, spine and wrist; weight-bearing and resistance exercise help maintain bone.',
'**Osteoarthritis (OA)** is the breakdown of **articular cartilage**, common in the knees, hips and hands, with pain that worsens with activity and stiffness under 30 minutes in the morning. **Rheumatoid arthritis (RA)** is an **autoimmune** inflammation of the **synovium**, usually symmetrical, with prolonged morning stiffness and possible deformities. Exercise, joint protection and weight management help both. **Low back pain** is extremely common; most cases improve with activity, exercise and education. **Herniated discs** can compress nerve roots, causing **sciatica**—pain radiating down the leg.',
label('joint','A synovial joint',[['cartilage','Articular cartilage','Wears down in osteoarthritis.'],['synovium','Synovial membrane','Inflamed in rheumatoid arthritis.'],['capsule','Joint capsule','Encloses the joint; tightens with immobility.'],['ligament','Ligament','Connects bone to bone; injured in sprains.'],['bone','Bone','Weakened in osteoporosis.'],['cavity','Joint cavity','Contains synovial fluid.']])]],
['Tissue healing',[
'Injured tissue heals in overlapping phases. The **inflammatory phase** (about the first **3–5 days**) brings redness, heat, swelling, pain and loss of function as the body clears debris; treatment focuses on protection, swelling control and gentle movement. The **proliferative (repair) phase** (about **day 3 to 3 weeks**) lays down new collagen and blood vessels; controlled loading helps fibers form properly. The **remodeling (maturation) phase** (about **3 weeks to a year or more**) strengthens and aligns collagen along lines of stress, so progressive strengthening and functional training matter.',
'Signs of overdoing it include pain lasting more than about 2 hours after exercise, increased swelling or loss of motion the next day. PTs adjust exercise **dosage**—intensity, sets, repetitions, frequency—based on the healing phase and the patient’s response.',
order('Phases of healing',['Inflammation (first days)','Proliferation / repair (days to weeks)','Remodeling / maturation (weeks to months)'])]],
['Types of therapeutic exercise',[
'**Passive range of motion (PROM)**: the clinician moves the joint with no muscle effort from the patient—used when the patient cannot or should not move. **Active-assisted ROM (AAROM)**: the patient moves with help. **Active ROM (AROM)**: the patient moves without help. **Resistive exercise**: the muscle works against resistance such as weights, bands or the clinician’s hand.',
'Resistive exercise can be **isometric**—the muscle **contracts without visible joint motion** (pushing against a wall, quad sets)—**isotonic**, in which the joint moves through range with a constant load, either **concentric** (muscle shortens, as when lifting a weight) or **eccentric** (muscle lengthens under control, as when lowering it), or **isokinetic**, performed at a **constant speed** on a special machine. **Progressive resistive exercise** gradually increases the load. Flexibility, balance, endurance, aerobic and functional training complete the exercise toolbox.',
"**Manual therapy** is another key intervention: **joint mobilization** (graded, skilled movements of a joint), **soft-tissue mobilization** and **manipulation** reduce pain and restore motion. **Open-chain** exercises move a limb freely in space (a seated knee extension), while **closed-chain** exercises keep the foot or hand fixed (a squat or wall push-up) and are often safer and more functional after knee injuries. **Patient education**—explaining the condition, pacing activity and teaching a home exercise program—helps patients manage their own recovery.",
match('Match the exercise type',[['PROM','Clinician moves the joint; no patient effort'],['AAROM','Patient moves with help'],['AROM','Patient moves without help'],['Isometric','Muscle contracts with no joint motion'],['Concentric','Muscle shortens under load'],['Eccentric','Muscle lengthens under control'],['Isokinetic','Constant speed on a machine'],['Progressive resistive','Load increases over time']])]],
['Joint replacement',[
'**Total knee arthroplasty (TKA)** and **total hip arthroplasty (THA)** replace damaged joint surfaces, usually because of severe OA. Rehabilitation starts within hours to a day after surgery: early walking with a walker, range of motion (knee **extension to 0°** and flexion to about **90° and beyond**), quadriceps strengthening and blood clot prevention with ankle pumps and mobility.',
'After a **posterior-approach THA**, surgeons may order **hip precautions** to prevent dislocation: **no hip flexion beyond 90 degrees**, **no crossing the legs (adduction past midline)** and **no turning the leg inward (internal rotation)**. Patients use raised toilet seats, reachers and long-handled sponges. Anterior-approach precautions differ (avoiding extension and external rotation), and some surgeons use none—always follow the surgeon’s orders.',
"After **total knee arthroplasty**, the most important early goal is full knee **extension**, because a knee that cannot straighten makes walking inefficient. Patients often use **cold** and elevation for swelling, a walker or cane for the first weeks, and progress to stationary cycling, step-ups and balance work. Signs of a **deep vein thrombosis**—calf pain, warmth, redness and swelling—or of infection must be reported to the surgeon right away.",
sort('Follows posterior hip precautions?',['Safe with posterior precautions','Violates posterior precautions'],[['Sitting on a raised toilet seat',0],['Crossing the operated leg over the other',1],['Using a reacher to pick up socks',0],['Bending forward to tie shoes',1],['Sleeping with a pillow between the knees',0],['Turning the toes of the operated leg inward',1]])]],
['Practice scenarios',[
kase('Choosing exercise',{a:{text:'A patient two days after knee surgery cannot yet lift her leg but can tighten her thigh muscle. Which exercise fits?',choices:[['Isometric quad sets','b','Correct. Contraction without joint motion protects healing tissue.',true],['Heavy squats','b','Too much load this early.',false]]},b:{text:'Three weeks later she has good motion. What progression makes sense?',choices:[['Progressive resistive exercise with bands and functional training','c','Correct. Remodeling needs progressive load.',true],['Stop exercising—she is healed','c','Tissue keeps remodeling for months.',false]]},c:{text:'You report her progress to the PT, who updates the plan of care.',end:true}}),
{t:'callout',kind:'exam',title:'Sample question',text:'HOSA asks what type of resistive exercise involves contracting a muscle without visible joint motion. The answer is **isometric**.'}]]
],takeaways:['Sprains injure ligaments and strains injure muscles or tendons (grades I–III); OA wears cartilage, RA inflames the synovium; osteoporosis raises fracture risk.','Healing moves from inflammation to proliferation to remodeling; exercise dosage follows the phase and the patient’s response.','PROM, AAROM, AROM and resistive exercise; isometric (no joint motion), isotonic (concentric and eccentric) and isokinetic (constant speed); posterior hip precautions: no flexion beyond 90°, no adduction past midline, no internal rotation.'],terms:[['Sprain','A stretch or tear of a ligament.'],['Isometric exercise','Muscle contraction without visible joint motion.'],['Arthroplasty','Surgical replacement of a joint.']],mcqs:[q('What type of resistive exercise involves contracting a muscle without visible joint motion?','Isometric','Active','Passive','Progressive','HOSA sample question.'),q('A sprain injures a:','Ligament','Muscle','Tendon only','Bone','Ligaments connect bone to bone.'),q('Osteoarthritis mainly damages:','Articular cartilage','Synovium only','Skin','Nerves','RA inflames the synovium.'),q('Lowering a dumbbell slowly is which contraction?','Eccentric','Concentric','Isometric','Isokinetic','The muscle lengthens.'),q('Which exercise is done at a constant speed on a machine?','Isokinetic','Isometric','PROM','AAROM','Constant velocity.'),q('Which is a posterior hip precaution?','No hip flexion beyond 90 degrees','Walk without a device immediately','Cross the legs often','Turn the leg inward','Prevents dislocation.'),q('What does the O in POLICE stand for?','Optimal','Oxygen','Orthotic','Only','Optimal loading.'),q('In which healing phase is collagen aligned along lines of stress?','Remodeling','Inflammation','Hemostasis','Proliferation only','Can last a year or more.')],frq:{prompt:'Compare osteoarthritis and rheumatoid arthritis, and explain how a PT would progress exercise for a patient after total knee replacement using the phases of healing and the types of therapeutic exercise.',rubric:[['OA vs RA',['cartilage','autoimmune','synovium','symmetrical','morning stiffness','weight-bearing joints']],['Healing phases',['inflammation','proliferation','remodeling','swelling','collagen']],['Exercise types',['prom','aarom','arom','isometric','resistive','concentric','eccentric']],['TKA application',['walker','extension','flexion','quad','ankle pumps','progress','functional']]],model:'Osteoarthritis is degeneration of the articular cartilage, most common in weight-bearing joints such as the knees and hips; pain increases with activity and morning stiffness usually lasts less than 30 minutes. Rheumatoid arthritis is an autoimmune disease that inflames the synovial membrane, often affects joints symmetrically, causes prolonged morning stiffness and can deform joints. After a total knee replacement for osteoarthritis, the inflammatory phase in the first days calls for protection and swelling control, walking with a walker, ankle pumps to prevent clots, isometric quad sets and gentle passive or active-assisted range of motion toward full extension and about 90 degrees of flexion. In the proliferative phase over the next weeks, the PT progresses to active range of motion and light resistive exercise, such as concentric and eccentric straight-leg raises and mini-squats, while watching for increased swelling or pain. In the remodeling phase, the PT uses progressive resistive exercise, balance and functional training such as stairs and sit-to-stand so that new collagen aligns with normal stresses and the patient can return to daily activities.'},sources:[RULES,NIAMS_OA,NIAMS_SPRAIN,AAOS_HIP,CHOOSEPT]};
